 very difficult and I think people  found at the most difficult task but  there was absolutely no interest in  anybody from the task force coming out  and saying that we should be keeping  everything because we can it was very  clear to the people who put this who  charged the task force that in fact  presidents of universities for example  would not  invest in digital preservation if they  thought it was a black hole and it would  be collecting everything  indiscriminately they wanted a lot of  work going into a selection which i  think is very smart there have to be  incentives the third is there have to be  incentives for decision-makers to make  the decision to preserve in the public  interest and I will talk a little bit  about incentives because it seems like a  sort of odd thing to talk about in  preservation and odd in terms of mission  work but in fact incentives are key to  actually getting things done there needs  to be the fourth is there need to be  needs to be appropriate organization and  governance of digital preservation  activities and I'll say that I'd be  surprised if this campus were any  different than any campus I've ever been  on where digital preservation happens in  multiple places and nobody knows where  they are nobody knows what other people  are doing and they don't even know about  the duplication of effort that they're  making so it is really critical that  there be a strategy for digital  preservation and that people know what  the strategy is and there be clear lines  of responsibility for for who does what  we heard from many government agencies  that produce public records that you  know or by law to be preserved when  somebody was ready to hand off a record  a digital record for example be he a  scientist in a laboratory or somebody in  immigration they returned to someone and  say was so where do I send this and  there was no answer nobody knew who was  in charge and so I think it's quite  essential that people beat asked  specifically and that second that  everybody knows who is tasked  specifically about who is in charge of  what it's the only way to actually  implement the strategy because it is  after all quite we're dealing with  ephemeral and distributed materials it's  not as if somebody can just put a whole  bunch of books in a room and say okay  let's take care of this it has to be  handled in a distributed way the fifth  challenge is the mechanisms for securing  ongoing and efficient allocation of  resources to digital preservation  activities efficient doesn't mean cheap  means that you get the biggest bang for  your buck so there's a there's an  appropriate amount of redundancy but  there's not unnecessary duplication not  surprisingly the things that people  found the greatest challenge about  meeting those conditions I shouldn't say  incidentally that the first year we  spent looking for somebody who could  claim that they had fulfilled all five  of those conditions and the closest we  got was somebody who thought they were  doing sort of three maybe four but then  on closer examination when we asked them  questions decided no they weren't  actually doing that many either so it's  complicated and it's not as if there's  there's a model out there that people  can hand off to somebody else getting  into conversation I think with people  other people who were doing digital  preservation is possibly the first  intelligent step but almost an order of  the challenges I'll tell you what they  were the first was uncertainty about  selection criteria which I mentioned for  assessing long-term value on the one  hand we had scientists talking about  large scale data sets some of them could  say well we know that those that you  know those petabytes of data from space  will not be useful after 10 years  because you know we'll have superseded  the observational data with something  else on the other hand there was a  different set of astronomers who said  you know what all has drawn all  astronomy astronomical data is  historical data we can't get rid of  anything so they were having a hard time  sorting through that one but they did  realize they had to make a choice so  they were thinking about ways to sample  historical data so that they could do it  cheaply we also found a great challenge  where the small handcrafted digital  collections many of which are bound on  campuses made by students and faculty  members and then the biggest one which  we decided we couldn't avoid is the  plethora of new genres that are emerging  on the wet web mediated by social media  and the so called open web things like  Wikipedia and Twitter things that we  know are of enormous social significance  but what part of it is significant how  we collected how we save it where the  copyright in  and ownership claims to it not a clue  absolutely not a clue I think it was on  the on the basis of the the complete  golf about how important it was versus  knowing what to do about it that really  prompted the library of congress to say  you know we're going to take the twitter  archive we don't know what that means we  have no idea what that means but Twitter  doesn't either and and who better to  find out with than twitter so i mean  that's the kind of partnership where you  can go into it with an expectation of  failure but it's a failure but the but  failures are learning experiences if its  failure without a learning experience  than it is really a failure but if you  go into something knowing that you'll  the outcome actually is knowledge not  necessarily saving something then you  can get pretty far with trying out  different methods for selection like I  tell tell you my colleagues at the  library that were absolutely gobsmacked  when the first public reaction besides  some people saying hey that's great  where people individually trying to  reach individuals in the library of  congress saying that twitter I want my  twitter back you know I didn't know that  it was going to be preserved forever I  never knew that it was you know then  anyone had a right to preserve it and  and of course it's all very clear on the  Twitter when you click through that  license that unless you make an  exemption you know it is actually it's  not your private property anymore and  and people publish this online and it's  totally accessible but they're very  sensitive about it being preserved so  you run into some very unexpected issues  simply by trying things out there's also  another challenge which I'll get into  it's quite specific in every community  the Scala community where a scientific  community or in Hollywood for example  but there's a really fundamental miss  misalignment of incentives to preserve  copyright law is completely out of whack  with what we need to be doing in terms  of copyrighted materials and the  the rewards in the Academy for attending  to digital preservation where it matters  the most are also out of whack and I  will actually spend some time with on  talking about that there and then  finally I want to say that the other  thing that was really baffling which I  don't think we're ever going to solve is  is trying to discriminate between  preservation the cost of making  something accessible versus the cost to  making something preserving something in  fact they're not separable and so  teasing out preservation costs as  opposed to access costs is a fool's game  I think it's much better to think of  long-term sustained data management in  which you look at every object as having  a life cycle and the life cycle can be  five years it can be one year and it can  be 50 years but it's much more useful to  think about life cycle costs and  lifecycle management of digital data and  it also makes it a whole lot easier to  think about selection in this in this  case the the really good news is we did  find that once the risks were identified  and we recommended everybody doing an  assessment and where they are with this  make us do a self assessment of where  they are based on some of the things in  our report we found that in fact for  nearly everything that we thought of as  a risk there is a mitigation there is  actually a lot to be done in order to  make the environment friendly for  digital preservation and the really good  news i think is that it is everybody's  problem it's not you know it's not yells  problem and it's not my problem it's  actually everybody's problem so when  rumor first spread that that the Library  of Congress and NSF we're looking to put  together this task force on economics we  had a flood of people volunteering time  and effort and money and a lot of  interest on the part of people in a  university administration of course they  also thought that we would hand them a  blueprint for what what is to be done  actually a business model as opposed to  an economic model but I think economic  models are business models are actually  pointless without our neck  model and we did get fairly far to  articulating several types of economic  models some for-profit and for the  for-profit sector and some  not-for-profit so the three things that  I want to say is the you know final  recommendations which I think are most  important for this community are and  they're they're just three and they're  not you know they're not easy is first  and foremost to articulate a compelling  value proposition for preservation I  said first that in the beginning that  people really do care about preservation  they just don't call it preservation  they call it access I think that  particularly in the preservation field  if you really want to get more resources  and the right kind of resources for work  start talking about the outcomes of  preservation start talking about the  things that you can do with preserved  assets or however you want to call them  in scientific world they can talk about  the promise of future productivity of  science how do we talk about that in the  library how do we actually talk about  the future productivity of our students  and our faculty well we talked about it  actually all the time that without the  collections research and teaching is  really engendered so talk first about  what the outcomes of preservation are  going to be and then talk about what it  takes to get there from here when the  value proposition of saving astronomical  data is going to be different from  saving feature films for example but  every audience has its own sort of sweet  spot in terms of what it really wants to  pay attention to but i think i would  start first with what are you what is  the benefit of preservation that's the  recognition of benefit on the part of  stakeholders the second thing is to is  to provide clear incentives to preserve  in the public interest what does that  mean to provide incentives unclear  incentives to preserve in the public  interest while providing incentives for  stakeholders as we say anybody who cares  about the outcome of preservation to  preserve digital assets directly or to  provide preservation services for others  and tailoring those incentives to the  prevailing community norms and to  information policy regulations and  privacy concerns is really what we're  talking about  we're talking about both creating  incentives positive things that get  people to preserve and also removing  disincentives of which there are many to  take a fairly simple example I'll take a  bench scientist at Yale University or  medical scientist at Yale one incentive  that a scientist has to to preserve  digital data is the possibility of  reusing the data if in fact they're  interested in sharing the data a much  stronger incentive would be if his grant  depended upon digital curation if the  grant actually stipulated that the data  had to be managed and archived and the  digital curation was part of the grant  and there was money in the grant for it  and there would be a penalty be paid if  curation didn't happen now that's really  an incentive it's one thing for NSF to  say everybody needs to save their data  and curate their data it's not but  that's not enough because I can't think  of as we actually asked people at the  foundation and they said well privately  they didn't know of any particular grant  which had been denied API because they  hadn't saved data from a previous grant  they give them a hard time maybe but  they didn't actually deny research funds  research funds is what researchers care  about so holding back money would be  very important a big advance is that NSF  is now saying that people have to have a  data management plan that's very good  nobody knows what a data management plan  is yet but I see this as an opportunity  for libraries frankly I mean how many of  you have gone over to to science  laboratories and say hey you've got a  data management plan you're worried  about maybe I can help you this is  really an opportunity to begin data  curation where it counts which is at the  bench I'd also say that there are  positive disincentives for scientists to  curate data and what are they well  because every every second every money  every penny that's not spent on research  it's really a demerit in his portfolio  because there is no reward in his reward  system or her reward system for curating  and preserving data it's just time away  from something that is rewarded so I  think the the incentives are really out  of  within the university itself that on the  one hand you reward people for creating  data on the other hand there's a  positive disincentive for people  preserving data and somehow they think  that all of some how things are going to  end up in the library sort of magically  packaged and ready for you to take care  of and serve up again this is a  fundamental misunderstanding data  curation really begins with the creator  and it should be in partnership with  librarians preservationist data  scientists informatics specialists  whatever you want to call them every  discipline will have a different name  but it's a partnership that begins at  the very beginning I think that that  libraries have well I should say  universities have a second opportunity  as well as a responsibility and that is  you have thousands of students coming  through here every year you teach them  literacy in many ways media literacy  reading literacy mathematical literacy  digital literacy is what is needed now  because our students are digital they've  grown up digital they know how to use  machines fluently many times a better  than we do we think we fear but that  doesn't mean that they're literate  necessarily and they know how to use  information well there's a chance that  and as well as an obligation that we can  take the current generation of sort of  web 2.0 Earth's sort of readwrite  culture of Technology and turn it into a  read/write curate and preserve culture I  think that everybody who goes through an  undergraduate program at any good  university college or actually it should  start in high school people should be  taught how to manage their own data  while they're creating it not just how  to create a paper or a project that gets  an A and it be judged only on the  intellectual merit but just as much as  you would give it a marriage for  plagiarism for example or worse than a  demerit not taking care of data properly  file naming formats and things like that  is something that people need to learn  essentially from the cradle because it's  not something that they're going to into  it until it's too late so finally let me  just end with a few  rotations to people particularly in the  library and particularly in preservation  I think that first I want to emphasize  again how crucial selection is I think  that archivists have a unique  perspective on dealing with abundance  and sorting through what's important to  document what's important to say what's  important to sample and I think  audio-visual archivists are particularly  adapted this I think we have a lot to  learn from them I also think that  preservation is I know this will sound  somewhat bold and even counterintuitive  in these budgetary times but I really do  believe passionately that preservation  is emerging as a keystone of the  University and its its social worth I  really think in this time when universe  universities feel besieged by so much  and their whole social mission is under  question from many sectors they can  claim rightly so that they contribute to  knowledge that they really contribute to  the knowledge economy but so do a lot of  other sectors a lot of private sectors  can contribute to knowledge as well the  one thing that the private sector will  never do and this was demonstrated  roundly by our economies they will never  care about preservation they will never  ensure the continuity of information  from one generation to another it is  impossible for market forces to actually  take care of that and so I think that of  all the things that libraries do on  University campuses I can I have heard  people on university's administration  speculating how all of them could go  away many of the things we love most  about libraries can very well be  imagined is being superseded by  commercial services frankly search for  example access for example but no one  believes that anybody but a university  is going to preserve and so I think it  means that this is really the Golden Age  of preservation that if the university  can really configure a lot of its  information culture around the creation  and curation of data that the library  really stands to benefit enormously it  means a lot of changes in the profession  of of preservation means working with a  lot of different skills and developing  different skill sets and we  have before but I really believe that it  is it really is the beginning of a  marvelous age for preservation  preservation the paradigm preservation  for the digital world is not about  fixity it's about incremental  responsibilities you can think long term  but really incrementally the key is to  think about handoffs secure handoffs  making sure that data is well curated  and making sure that it is handed off to  somebody who can curate it and stored it  in the next step I think you have a lot  of skills on campus that are very good  at this I think there are many people at  the by Nikki who know precisely how to  identify really important content  creators and collectors of content how  to develop relationships with them and  how to ensure that their collection of  information comes to the by Nikki when  they are ready to move it to a  stewardship organization take that  paradigm move it online go to digital  creators and I think you have exactly  the same thing going on you know what is  it that makes this obligation for  preservation so important I think it's  important for us not just as individuals  and as families and as researchers at  universities but I think it is really  vital for us as a species as Homo  sapiens and I mean that quite literally  we differ from our fellow species in  many ways but scientists are telling us  every day we differ a lot less from our  fellow species than we think but there's  one thing incontestably we differ from  all of the species with and that is the  ability to create artificial memory to  actually embody knowledge in external  forms that we then can pass on across  time across space and across multiple  generations we are the only species that  does that and we do it through  preservation so I thank you very much  as you can tell I think this is the  beginning of a very exciting time for  preservation and I know that I have  given a lot of information not nearly as  much as I would have liked to of course  but I would commend to you the two  reports of the task force and I would  simply I would say just Google  sustainable economics digital planet  that's a lot easier than trying to find  this the URL and it's it's the reports  were actually on the super San Diego  super supercomputer site and i also want  to recommend to you the Library of  Congress that has just published its its  10-year review of its end it program and  it's now online digital preservation gov  the ww preservation gov and there's a  full discussion about the many different  models of shared stewardship there that  I commend to you they're very frank  discussions of the various efforts that  were tried some failed but in the end  succeeded
 

